1.3 The file header works in one direction only
----------------------------------------

    The file header holds in one direction only, and the two directions are
    shaped very differently:

      - **carries the header => belongs to the AGPL layer** -- holds, and it
        has been checked in full against the authoritative test (whether the
        file exists in the unmodified `base-snapshot/v2.46.0` snapshot): of
        the 647 files carrying the header, **not one** is a counterexample
        (measured at commit `d08b16c3`, which added `docs/dev/spdx-backfill-retro.md`). That half of the test is therefore an
        observation rather than a stipulation, and the two tests agree
        completely in this direction. It is falsifiable all the same --
        someone putting the header on a base file by mistake would produce a
        counterexample -- which makes it more useful as a **check** than as an
        assertion: the command below is the check for "has anyone stuck an
        AGPL header onto a base file", and it passes only when it reports
        `基座文件误贴 0` (0 base files mis-stamped).

            bun run check:license-headers

        This is no longer an inline pipeline either: the same gate as the
        reporting command above checks all three directions in one run. It
        first prints an **environment line** (the test's snapshot tag, the
        snapshot-tree file count, the tracked-file count, and the count on
        Qianmo's side), then a **determination line**. The figures on the
        environment line move whenever files are added or removed, so they are
        deliberately not copied here -- run the gate to see them. The measured
        determination line follows; the gate output is in Chinese, and all
        three values must be zero:

            [license-headers] OK：阡陌自有缺头 0（另有 83 个在豁免表内），基座文件误贴 0，豁免文件被盖章 0

        **The gate checks three things, which are not the same as the two
        inference directions above**: (1) a **missing header** -- a Qianmo-side
        file not in the exemption table whose first five lines lack either
        required line; (2) a **mis-stamped base file** -- an SPDX line in the
        first five lines of a file that is present in the base snapshot, the
        counterexample discussed here; and (3) a **stamped exempt file**. The
        four files named above, together with `Cargo.lock` (five named
        exemptions), deliberately carry no header rather than merely being
        unable to carry one, so the exemption is two-way: a header is neither
        required nor permitted. Stamping `LICENSE.base` with an AGPL line is
        exactly the **misstatement** described above. **One exception**:
        `NOTICE` itself is forbidden an SPDX line but not a copyright line --
        its line 2 `Copyright 2026 Qianmo AgentNest Team` is the copyright
        notice that belongs on the licensing notice itself.

        **One more distinction prevents two counts from being conflated**:
        the gate calls a file "header-bearing" only when **both lines** appear
        within its first five lines, whereas the `git grep … | awk -F:
        '$2<=5'` command in section 1.5 below counts the SPDX line alone. They
        currently give the same result (there are no files missing the
        copyright line), but their definitions differ and would diverge if a
        file carried only the SPDX line; run each to compare them.

      - **no file header, therefore the base MIT layer** -- **does not hold**.
        The reason is no longer that a batch of headers is outstanding, but
        the 83 header-less files above that are absent from the snapshot tree:
        **82 of them are counterexamples**. **The remaining one is
        `LICENSE.base`, and it is not a counterexample** -- it carries no
        header, and its contents are the upstream MIT text verbatim, so by
        content it does sit in the MIT layer and the inference happens to land
        correctly on it. That is a coincidence rather than the test working:
        both tests read the path, not the contents (see the counting sentence
        above and the "What that test cannot answer" paragraph later in this
        section). It is also the only such case among the 83 -- comparing
        contents file by file, it is the one that is byte-for-byte identical
        to a file in the snapshot. There is a second and finer failure too:
        the 180 modified base files described in the next bullet **do** belong
        to the base layer, yet the Qianmo changes inside them do not become
        MIT on that account -- layering by provenance is not the same as
        licensing by line. (What license those files are distributed under is
        stated in the next bullet.)

1.4 What that test cannot answer
----------------------------------------

    The authoritative test is whether the file exists in the `base-snapshot/*`
    snapshot; see section 2 below. **What that test cannot answer is set out
    here as well, so that it is not taken for a licensing conclusion**: it
    answers "was this file imported along with the base project", **not**
    "under what license is this file granted". `LICENSE.base` is a standing
    counterexample -- the path was added by Qianmo and is absent from the
    snapshot, so the authoritative test places it in Qianmo's AGPL layer, while
    its contents are the upstream MIT license text verbatim; **neither test
    gives the right answer for that file**, which is exactly why it is one of
    the four "deliberately no header" entries above. A second mismatch has to
    be stated as plainly: the same test places 27 `.jpg` and 3 `.png`
    **non-code assets** in Qianmo's layer, and **AGPL-3.0 is a software
    license**. The test does not determine the licenses for those works; they
    are instead classified by use: project-made works are granted under
    CC BY-SA 4.0, the third-party mark is not granted, and the application
    documents are All rights reserved (removed from the current version on
    2026-09-26). Section 1.7 below gives the
    complete inventory, the basis for each class, and the disclosure for one
    AI-generated asset.

